How Each System Works in Flight
1. Solar Energy System ☀️ (40 kW)
In-Flight Operation:
Sunlight hits aircraft
        ↓
200 m² solar panels on wings & fuselage
        ↓
Photovoltaic cells convert light → electricity
        ↓
DC power (400-800V) generated
        ↓
Sent to IEMS for distribution
Flight Conditions:
Daytime (optimal): Full 40 kW generation
Dawn/Dusk: 20-30 kW (reduced angle)
Cloudy: 15-25 kW (diffuse light)
Night: 0 kW (battery mode)
Key Advantage: Works best at high altitude (12 km) where:
No clouds blocking sunlight
Stronger solar radiation
Longer daylight hours
2. Wind Energy System 🌬️ (20 kW)
In-Flight Operation:
Aircraft moves through air at 648 km/h
        ↓
20 small turbines on wing edges
        ↓
Airflow spins turbine blades
        ↓
Generators convert rotation → electricity
        ↓
AC power converted to DC
        ↓
Sent to IEMS
Flight Conditions:
High speed (600+ km/h): Full 20 kW
Cruise (400-600 km/h): 15 kW
Low speed (<400 km/h): 8-10 kW
Stationary: 0 kW
Key Advantage: The faster the falcon flies, the more wind energy it generates!
Smart Design:
Turbines are retractable (fold in during takeoff/landing)
Automatically deploy at cruise altitude
Minimal drag impact (aerodynamic design)
3. Water/Hydraulic Energy System 💧 (15 kW)
In-Flight Operation:
500 liters of water in closed-loop system
        ↓
Solar heat concentrators heat water to 150°C
        ↓
High-pressure steam created
        ↓
Steam spins turbine
        ↓
Generator produces electricity
        ↓
Steam condenses back to water (no loss!)
        ↓
Water returns to reservoir
        ↓
Cycle repeats
Flight Conditions:
Full sun: 15 kW (optimal heating)
Partial sun: 10 kW
Cloudy: 5-8 kW (residual heat)
Night: 3-5 kW (stored thermal energy)
Key Advantage:
Closed-loop = no water loss
Thermal storage provides power even after sunset
Works as backup when solar is reduced
4. Sky/Ionospheric Energy System ⚡ (5 kW)
In-Flight Operation:
Atmosphere has electrical potential difference
        ↓
Ground level: 0V
12 km altitude: +100,000V
        ↓
Conductive antennas on aircraft collect charge
        ↓
Voltage regulator converts to usable power
        ↓
Sent to IEMS
Flight Conditions:
High altitude (12 km): Full 5 kW
Medium altitude (6-10 km): 3-4 kW
Low altitude (<6 km): 1-2 kW
Ground: 0 kW
Key Advantage:
Works 24/7 (day and night)
Increases with altitude
Free atmospheric electricity

